 **Sign-in sources.** The saved session (`internal/auth`) comes first. If there is none, or it has expired, the app migrates the login of another client on this computer (`internal/migrate`): Baidu's own Linux client first (its Electron cookie store, read with a small pure-Go SQLite reader), then BaiduPCS-Go's active account (`internal/pcsimport`, reading `pcs_config.json`). It skips this when the user signed out on purpose (`settings.SkipImport`). The login screen also offers it as a link. Every sign-in is saved at once, and saved again when `Client.Credentials` changes (the uid found, cookies the netdisk rotated; `Client.OnChange`) and on quit.
 
+**Android client (`android/`).** A second product in the same repository: a Kotlin + Jetpack Compose Material 3 app built with Gradle (AGP 9.4.1, Kotlin 2.4.20, Compose BOM 2026.09.00, compileSdk 37, minSdk 26), entirely separate from `go build`. It shares no code with the desktop app but speaks the same netdisk protocol (the pan.baidu.com web endpoints plus `/api/precreate` → `pcs/superfile2` parts → `/api/create`), the same backup layout and the same `.gpg` format, so the two halves back up into one netdisk folder. `:core` (pure JVM, `com.pengstem.baidunetgo.core`) holds `net/BaiduApi` (client, chunked upload, QR sign-in), `crypto/Pgp` (BouncyCastle bcpg; BouncyCastle's v5 session-key quirk had to be turned off for gpg/Go compatibility) and `backup/` (the incremental engine over a `Netdisk` interface and a `BackupSource`, so MediaStore albums and SAF trees are just sources); `:app` (`com.pengstem.baidunetgo`) holds the Compose screens, MediaStore album scanning, the SAF folder picker, Keystore-encrypted storage, WorkManager periodic workers and the foreground backup service. `android/docs/CORE-API.md` is the contract between them. Tests: `./gradlew :core:test` runs the client against `cmd/fakepand` (the same `internal/fakepan`, started as a process and its `url=…` line read from stdout; `FAKEPAN_URL` reuses one) and OpenPGP against the real `gpg` in both directions, and `:app:testDebugUnitTest` holds codec/path tests plus Robolectric renders written to `android/screenshots/` (there is no emulator here: no `/dev/kvm`). Builds need `ANDROID_HOME`, `JAVA_HOME` (JDK 21) and a writable `GRADLE_USER_HOME`; `android/keystore/dev.jks` is a public dev-only keystore for `assembleDebug`. `docs/ANDROID.md` is the hand-over (build, CI notes, update-channel idea).
+
 **One keyring key for every build.** `mygo.Secrets` names its service after the build (`<identifier>.dev` under `mygo dev`, the app name under `go run`, the identifier when packaged), so `OpenStore` pins the key to `com.pengstem.baidunetgo`. `auth.Store.Older` lists the old services: `Load` tries their keys and saves the session again under the pinned one.
 
 **Fake netdisk (`internal/fakepan`).** An `httptest` server that emulates every web endpoint the client uses, over an in-memory tree with generated images, a PDF and a WAV. App UI tests (`app/*_test.go`, helper `signedIn` in `app/app_test.go`) drive real views with `ui.Tester` against it. When adding a new API call, add its endpoint to fakepan too.
@@ -88,6 +94,8 @@ Entering the page reads the cache again only when it is entered (`dedupEnter` no
 
 **Transfers (`internal/transfer`).** `Manager` is a queue run by `Parallel`. Downloads fetch 4 MB blocks over `Threads` connections into `<file>.bngdl`, with a `.bngdl.json` sidecar of finished blocks for resume. Task state persists to `transfers.json` in the user data dir. `OnChange`/`OnFinish` are called outside the manager's lock and read tasks back through `Tasks()`. Don't call them while holding `m.mu`: that deadlocked once.
 
+**Automatic backups (`internal/backup`, `internal/pgp`, `app/backup.go`).** The settings page's 备份 section mirrors chosen local folders into a netdisk folder (`<dest>/<folder name>/<relative path>`), incrementally: `backup.Run` walks a folder, skips a file whose size and mtime are what `backup.State` records under its absolute local path, creates the remote folders it needs through the small `backup.Client` interface (`List`/`Mkdir`/`Upload`, adapted from `*baidu.Client` in `app/backup.go`), uploads up to `Parallel` files at once with the app's upload connection count, and reports progress from worker goroutines (the app coalesces it through `a.update`). Encrypted runs stream each file through `pgp.Encrypt` into a temp file in `os.TempDir()`, upload it as `<name>.gpg`, and remove it even on failure. The engine never deletes anything, collects per-file errors instead of aborting, and leaves a failed file out of the state so the next run retries it. State lives in `backup-state.json` under the user data dir (`backupStoreDir` points tests at a temp folder; nothing is written when signed in to the fake netdisk); the passphrase is not in `settings.json` but sealed with a keyring key in `backup-key.enc` (`backup-key.key` when there is no keyring) by `app/backup_key.go`, a second file/key pair so that signing out — which clears the session's — does not lose it. `app/backup.go` schedules runs (`time.AfterFunc` → `a.update`, first run a couple of minutes after launch, then every interval, one at a time, cancelled on sign-out/quit/settings change) and `app/backup_page.go` draws the section. `internal/pgp` encrypts with a passphrase only — AES-256, iterated+salted SHA-256 S2K, no compression, MDC — so `gpg -d file.gpg` opens the backup on any machine; the Android client writes the same format. `docs/BACKUP.md` is the user-facing description.
+
 **Session storage (`internal/auth`).** Credentials are AES-GCM encrypted in `session.enc` under the user data dir. The key lives in `mygo.Secrets` (the system keyring), with a 0600 key-file fallback. macOS ties the keychain's "Always Allow" to the code signature, and an ad-hoc signature changes with every build, so each rebuild asks again unless it is signed with a fixed identity (`-sign`). A real identity turns on the hardened runtime, under which wazero's compiled code (PDFium) is killed: `entitlements.plist` grants `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` for it. Settings are plain JSON (`app/settings.go`).
 
 ## MyGo UI gotchas found here
@@ -100,6 +108,7 @@ Entering the page reads the cache again only when it is entered (`dedupEnter` no
 - The grid view is not `ui.GridView`: GridView builds each line as an element, so a tile that a resize moves to another line is built anew and jumps. `app/grid.go` positions tiles itself, all under one parent keyed by fsid (percent `Left`/`Width`, so the width never lags), and on a cell change `Jump`s the slot's offset to where it showed and `AnimateTo`s it back to 0. Element `Transition`s don't help here: MyGo skips layout transitions on frames where the window resized.
 - The folder's actions live in a toolbar on the path bar (`app/browser.go`'s `filesToolbar`): a new folder (`folder-plus`), the sort menu, the view switch, the clipboard-link button, refresh, and the upload pill at the end; the folder's own name and search sit before them, and the button's box is where a folder made from it flies from (`listing.makerAt`). The maker (`app/newfolder.go`) is no longer a place for buttons: the grid's last tile is a whole "新建" tile that makes a folder on a click (a plus, or an upload while files are dragged over the window, `listing.over`), and the list's maker row is only the drop hint, in the accent color saying where dropped files will go, built only while files are dragged so it takes no room at the foot of a full folder. An empty folder says "这里还空着" and offers one upload of its own, labelled apart from the toolbar's. A new folder is named in a draft row at the top (`draftID`, put back each frame by `placeDraft`); a click away with its name untouched gives up. Cmd-Shift-N starts it from the toolbar's button box.
 - Theme: the look is a native macOS app's, modeled on Pulse (egoist's MyGo app): the sidebar draws no background so the window's `VibrancySidebar` material shows (`main.go`: `TitleBarHiddenInset`, 52 px title bar), pages marked by white glyphs on colored tiles, the chosen one on a solid accent backdrop, a value at each row's end (`navValue`); the pane is white with a title over a subtitle (`pageTitle`), round gray tool buttons (`toolButton`), capsule segmented controls (`segmented`/`segmentedOf`), which fold into pop-up buttons (`choice`/`popUp`) when the title bar is narrower than `compactBar` (`a.compact`, from its last width; in a folder the tools fold instead whenever its whole path would not fit beside them open, `foldTools`, so the path never folds while they could give way, and the folder's title bar has no subtitle, the status bar counting its items), striped 28 px list rows (`stripe`), rounded gray `group` panels for settings and summaries, and the system typeface (no embedded fonts; `figure` sets tabular digits). Waits that fill a page or stand for a task use `plugins/loading` (a folder's `Ring`, the speed test's `Radar`; the boot screen shows no wait, only the bare window); small ones in buttons, rows and dialogs keep AppKit's `ui.Spinner`. `applyTheme` swaps in the app's light/dark `ui.Theme` each frame, its interface font coming from `internal/fontconfig` (see above). Extra colors are in `palette`. Motion is one curve and three lengths (`motionHover` 120 ms, `motionFade` 160 ms, `motionPanel` 180 ms), plus `popIn` and `rowEnter`, all in `app/theme.go`; no springs or bounce.
+- Measured frame costs for a 2 000-file folder, what was changed and what it bought, plus the remaining hot spots outside the files those changes touched: `docs/PERFORMANCE.md` (benchmarks in `app/bench_test.go`; `ui.Tester` frames are the measuring tool).
 - Shaders (`ui/shader`, sources in `app/shaders/*.kage`, wiring in `app/effects.go`): each shader draw, effect layer or gradient with stops is a "general" GPU instance that the window does not batch, so dozens of them cost frames. Measure with `MYGO_FRAME_STATS=all` (it counts instances by class); draw many small things as plain fills, and keep a shader's per-pixel loop short (`specks.kage` searches only the cells whose specks can reach the pixel, from one hash each, and returns at once outside their reach). Shaders and `Element.Effect` run interpreted on the CPU in `ui.Tester`, so the app uses them only when `a.gpu` (`p.Accelerated()`), with plain fallbacks the tests see.
 - `Painter.AnimationFrame` (and `After`) only paints the window again: the view is not built, so anything computed while building (an element's size, an effect's uniforms, state moved on in `View`) stands still in those frames. Read the time as you paint (`p.Now()` in a `Draw` callback, or in an effect's `PaintEffect`, as `specksFx` does), and call `c.Invalidate()` while something built must move (`a.moving()`). Stamp an animation's start in the first frame that shows it, not from the last frame built, which may be long past.
 - Files come and go through specks (`app/arrivals.go`): a deleted file stays in its listing while it dissolves (`a.dying`, kept through reloads by `keepDying`), and an upload shows in its folder at once as a row with a negative FsID that gathers as its bytes go up (`a.gathers`, by the path of the entry it makes); rows with a negative FsID or dissolving are never chosen or opened.
